  }
 ],
 "formulas": [
  {
   "title": "Gay-Lussac's (pressure) law",
   "tex": "\\role{th}{\\dfrac{P_1}{T_1}} = \\role{th}{\\dfrac{P_2}{T_2}}",
   "text": "At constant volume, P/T stays the same. T must be in kelvin."
  }
 ],
 "challenges": [
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "type": "number",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "A rigid cylinder holds a gas at **2 atm** and **300 K**. It is heated to **450 K**. What is the new pressure in atm?",
   "answer": 3,
   "hint": "Use \\( P_1/T_1 = P_2/T_2 \\).",
   "explain": "\\( 2/300 = P_2/450 \\), so \\( P_2 = 3 \\) atm.",
   "tolerance": 0.1,
   "unit": "atm"
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "type": "number",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "The gas shows **1 P₀** at 300 K. At what temperature (kelvin) will it show **2 P₀**?",
   "answer": 600,
   "hint": "Pressure is proportional to the kelvin temperature.",
   "explain": "To double the pressure at constant volume, double the kelvin temperature: **600 K**.",
   "tolerance": 5,
   "unit": "K",
   "set": {
    "T": 300
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "Why does the pressure of a gas in a rigid box rise on heating?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "The molecules get bigger"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "The molecules move faster and hit the walls harder and more often"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "More molecules enter the box"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "The box gets heavier"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "b",
   "hint": "Think about the kinetic picture.",
   "explain": "The number of molecules and the volume are fixed, but faster molecules hit the walls more often and with more force."
  }
 ],
 "takeaways": [
  "**Gay-Lussac's law:** at constant volume, \\( P/T = \\text{constant} \\) (T in kelvin).",
  "The P–T graph is a **straight line through the origin**.",
  "Heating at constant volume does **no work** (nothing moves), but raises the pressure."
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "Connects to",
   "text": "This is the **isochoric process** (constant volume): W = 0, so all the heat added raises the internal energy (Q = ΔU). All three laws combine into the **ideal gas equation**."
  }
 ]
}